== SCOPE WIDENED (mayor, 2026-10-05 10:5x AUSEST) ==
The skillrows worker found the same id-unaware lift in migration/from-re-frame-v1/README.md's M-70 "All three become" block (about L2834): `(rf/reg-interceptor :progress/with-completion {:doc ...} mw/with-progress-completion)` throws :rf.error/invalid-interceptor at load unless the value's own :id already equals the registration id. Its JVM control reproduced that error with `(def progress {:id :enrich ...}) (rf/reg-interceptor :app/progress progress)`. This bead's PR (worker/skillrows) also fixes that block: one comment line stating the id-match requirement, plus a pointer to the skill's case split. M-70's "What to do" fresh-descriptor block carries no :id and is unaffected.
== RESULT (worker/skillrows, PR #10898 — https://github.com/day8/re-frame2/pull/10898) ==
Passages: skills/re-frame-migration/references/auto-cross-cutting.md, §M-70:
- intro: the registration id must agree with the value's own :id;
- the example block: states the preserving-case assumption its REWRITEs rely on;
- the "The rewrite is mechanical" sentence becomes a three-case check. (1) Read the value's own :id. (2) Preserving case (a stable, unique :id equal to the registration id): lift mechanically. (3) Missing, mismatched or shared generated :id: stop, Type B. Link MIGRATION.md M-70's naming half and orchestrating-a-large-migration.md §2 (one registration per id), and require an approved plan that keeps chain order, function identity/behaviour, attachment scope and the definition-load obligation. Never silently choose names, strip or replace ids, or register distinct values under their shared constructor :id;
- "a clean compile is not acceptance".
The heading and its anchor are unchanged. The mayor widened the README hold: MIGRATION.md M-70's "All three become" block gains a comment that the value's :id must already equal :progress/with-completion, plus a link to the skill's case split.
Source: interceptor_registry.cljc reg-interceptor*. When (:id descriptor) is non-nil and not= the registration id, it calls throw-interceptor-id-mismatch!, which raises :rf.error/invalid-interceptor.
Executed (fresh JVM, public API, exit 0):
- (def progress {:id :enrich ...}) (rf/reg-interceptor :app/progress progress) -> THROW :rf.error/invalid-interceptor (the old recipe's shape: the red);
- {:id :app/audit ...} under :app/audit -> registers, and the chain runs [:audit];
- two distinct values from one constructor (:id :legacy/after): first registered, :ev/one runs [:first]; the second registered under the same id -> :ev/one now runs [:second], a silent overwrite;
- the second value under a new distinct id -> THROW :rf.error/invalid-interceptor.

